            'DIVINE KINGSHIP: The Pharaoh was the state\'s supreme ruler and living god (embodiment of Horus during life, Osiris after death). The Pharaoh\'s ritual correctness was believed to maintain Ma\'at (cosmic order) including the Nile flood and social harmony. Disobedience to the Pharaoh was religious transgression. This divine status provided powerful ideological support for royal authority.\n\nBUREAUCRACY: Below the Pharaoh was an elaborate bureaucracy with a Vizier as chief minister. Administration included treasury officials, granary officials managing grain storage, public works officials overseeing irrigation and construction, and military commanders. This system enabled Egypt to govern despite its vast size.\n\nSOCIAL HIERARCHY: From top to bottom: Pharaoh and royal family; priests and nobles; scribes (educated literate administrators); artisans and craftsmen; farmers (bulk of population); slaves. Scribal literacy was crucial—written knowledge of administration and records was the practical basis of state power.\n\nPOLITICAL PERIODS: Egyptian history divides into Old Kingdom (c. 2686-2181 BCE) with highly centralized state; Middle Kingdom (c. 2055-1650 BCE) with greater regional power; and New Kingdom (c. 1550-1070 BCE) as the imperial era. Between kingdoms, Intermediate Periods showed political fragmentation. This system maintained recognizable forms for 3,000 years.', page: 35,
