          id: '103_2_8_5',
          question: 'Describe the art and architecture of ancient Egypt.',
          answer: 'Egyptian art and architecture—spanning over 3,000 years from the Early Dynastic period to the Ptolemaic era—followed consistent aesthetic principles that conveyed religious meaning, royal power, and cosmic order through a visual language of extraordinary stability and sophistication.\n\nEgyptian artistic conventions were governed by the principle of conceptual representation: artists depicted what they knew rather than what they saw. Human figures followed the "Law of Frontality"—the head and legs shown in profile, the shoulders and eye shown frontally—to show all important features simultaneously. Size conveyed status: pharaohs were depicted larger than nobles, nobles larger than servants. This convention persisted for 3,000 years because it was not aesthetic convention but religious requirement—art served to preserve and activate divine power.\n\nMonumental architecture: the Egyptian achievement in stone construction was unmatched in the ancient world. The pyramids at Giza (c. 2560–2490 BCE)—the Great Pyramid of Khufu, Khafre\'s Pyramid, and Menkaure\'s Pyramid—are the only surviving wonders of the ancient world. Built as royal tombs, they align with astronomical precision (the Great Pyramid\'s sides are aligned within fractions of a degree to true north). The construction required quarrying, transporting, and precisely placing approximately 2.3 million stone blocks averaging 2.5 tonnes each.\n\nTemples: Great Egyptian temples—Karnak (dedicated to Amun-Ra), Luxor, and Abu Simbel (Ramesses II\'s rock-cut temple)—featured massive pylons (gateway towers), hypostyle halls filled with forest-like columns, sanctuaries for the divine image, and walls covered with hieroglyphic texts and carved narrative scenes of royal and divine activity.\n\nSculpture: Egyptian sculpture combined idealized beauty with formulaic conventions. The Great Sphinx at Giza (c. 2530 BCE) is the world\'s largest single-stone sculpture.',
