          id: '103_2_8_6',
          question: 'Describe the Persian Empire and its administrative and cultural achievements.',
          answer: 'The Persian (Achaemenid) Empire (c. 550–330 BCE), founded by Cyrus the Great and reaching its greatest extent under Darius I and Xerxes, was the largest empire the world had seen to that point—stretching from Egypt and Greece in the west to the Indus Valley in the east—and demonstrated remarkable administrative sophistication for a pre-modern state.\n\nCyrus the Great (r. 559–530 BCE) founded the empire through a series of rapid conquests: Media, Lydia, and Babylon fell in quick succession. His administrative innovation was equally significant: unlike Assyrian conquerors who terrorized conquered peoples, Cyrus permitted conquered peoples to practice their own religions and preserve their cultural traditions. His Cylinder (a clay cylinder now in the British Museum) records his policy of religious tolerance and the repatriation of exiled peoples—including allowing the Babylonian Exile Jews to return to Judah and rebuild their temple.\n\nDarius I (r. 522–486 BCE) consolidated and organized the empire. He divided it into provinces (satrapies) administered by appointed governors (satraps) accountable to the king, creating an effective system for governing a diverse empire. The Royal Road—a 2,700 km highway from Susa to Sardis with relay stations—enabled rapid communication and military movement. A standardized coinage (gold darics) facilitated trade. The magnificent palace complex at Persepolis—with its reliefs showing tribute-bearing representatives of all subject peoples—expressed both the empire\'s diversity and its unity under Persian rule.\n\nZoroastrianism, the Persian state religion founded by Zarathustra (Zoroaster), introduced the concept of cosmic dualism (Good/Ahura Mazda vs. Evil/Angra Mainyu), afterlife judgment, and apocalyptic eschatology—ideas that significantly influenced Judaism, Christianity, and Islam.',
